  {
   "id": "44pnY7TtAQAJ",
   "from": "kbemis",
   "author": "6033d3c0e7d242fa",
   "date": "2019-01-30T20:34:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: BiocParallel errors while normalizing",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">You have a couple options. Your spectra are currently an on-disk &#39;matter&#39; matrix, so they are not loaded into memory.<div><br></div><div>The default for on-disk data is to treat it as read-only, which is why your first version has an error.<div><br></div><div>If the dataset is small enough to load into memory, then you can simply do:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])[</span><span style=\"color:#008\">is</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">na</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span></div></code></div><div><br></div>(You can use iData() rather than spectra() if you prefer. They are the same.)</div><div><br></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><font face=\"arial, sans-serif\">Trying to assign into</font></span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> iData</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">data</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(102,102,0)\">[[</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(0,102,102)\">3</span><span style=\"font-family:monospace;color:rgb(102,102,0)\">]])[] </span><font color=\"#000000\" face=\"arial, sans-serif\">will not work in your example, because you are returning an in-memory matrix, but not assigning it to a variable, so no data ends up changing. You need to assign the in-memory matrix back into the imaging data slot to be able to modify it this way.</font><br></div><div><br></div><div>If you do not want to load the data into memory and don&#39;t mind modifying the original on-disk data (presumably an imzML file), you can do:<br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font><span style=\"color:#008\">readonly</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><font color=\"#000088\"><span style=\"color:#000\">FALSE<br><br></span></font></font><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ncol</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></font><span style=\"color:#000\">    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">if</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> anyNA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]][,</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span></font><font color=\"#666600\"><span style=\"color:#660\">])</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>        spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])[</span><span style=\"color:#008\">is</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">na</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]][,</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span></font><font color=\"#666600\"><span style=\"color:#660\">]),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>(The next version of matter will support linear indexing so you will no longer need a &#39;for&#39; loop, but a &#39;for&#39; loop may be more efficient in this case anyway, depending on the size of the data.)</div><div><br></div><div>-Kylie</div></div><div><br></div><div>Edit: I will also change the normalize method to work with missing values in the next version. Missing values in spectra tends to break a lot of other things which is why it hasn&#39;t come up before.</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "JUTmMXUGCAAJ",
   "from": "quantum suicide",
   "author": "bb4a45d0dd2b038e",
   "date": "2019-01-31T08:00:41Z",
   "subject": "Re: BiocParallel errors while normalizing",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Dear Kylie,</div><div><br></div><div>man thanks for your advice. It works :)</div><div><br></div><div>I am now using this import check on NAs:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> seq</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> length</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">if</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">any</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">is</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">na</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">iData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])[]))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">==</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">as</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]])[</span><span style=\"color:#008\">is</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">na</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">spectra</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]))]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div></div><div><br><br></div></div>"
